00:35:23,076 --> 00:35:29,076
Rambo is a 2008 American war action film co-written
and directed by Sylvester Stallone.

11
00:38:55,384 --> 00:39:01,384
A sequel to Rambo III (1988), it is the fourth installment in the Rambo film
series based on the character created by author David Morrell.

12
00:42:27,692 --> 00:42:33,692
Stallone stars as John Rambo, who leads a group of mercenaries into Burma to rescue
Christian missionaries, who have been kidnapped by a local infantry unit.

13
00:46:00,000 --> 00:46:06,000
The film co-stars Julie Benz, Paul Schulze, Matthew Marsden, Graham McTavish, Rey
Gallegos, Tim Kang, Jake La Botz, Maung Maung Khin, and Ken Howard.

14
00:49:32,307 --> 00:49:38,307
Rambo is dedicated to the memory of Richard Crenna, who had played
Colonel Sam Trautman in the previous films and died in 2003.

15
00:53:04,615 --> 00:53:10,615
The rights to the Rambo franchise were sold to Miramax Films
in 1997 after Carolco Pictures went bankrupt.

16
00:56:36,923 --> 00:56:42,923
Miramax intended to produce a fourth film but Stallone
was unmotivated to reprise the role.

17
01:00:09,230 --> 01:00:15,230
The rights were then sold to Nu Image and Millennium Films in 2005, who
green-lit the film before the release of Rocky Balboa (2006).

18
01:03:41,538 --> 01:03:47,538
Filming began in Thailand, Mexico, and the United
States in January 2007, and ended that May.

19
01:07:13,846 --> 01:07:19,846
Rambo was theatrically released in the United States by Lionsgate Films and the
Weinstein Company on January 25, 2008, and in Germany by Warner Bros.

20
01:10:46,153 --> 01:10:52,153
Pictures on February 14.

21
01:14:18,461 --> 01:14:24,461
It received mixed reviews, with praise aimed at Stallone’s direction and performance, action sequences, and
musical score, but criticism for its plot, excessively graphic violence, and political commentary.

22
01:17:50,769 --> 01:17:56,769
At the box office, it grossed $113.2 million worldwide against
a production budget between $47.5–50 million.

23
01:21:23,076 --> 01:21:29,076
The film was followed by Rambo: Last Blood, released in 2019.

